---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Contact from "../components/Contact.astro";
import { site } from "../data/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Privacy Policy"
  description="What Hatchfire and playhatchfire.com collect about you, and why. The short version: you can play without an account, and online play keeps only what it needs."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="container">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p>Last updated: {site.legalUpdated}</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-body">
    <div class="container prose">
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>The short version.</strong> You can play without an account, and then
          your save stays on your phone. If you go online (cloud saves, Waymeet,
          friends), our game server keeps your guild and what other players need to
          see, and nothing more. There are no ads, no analytics and no tracking. You can
          delete your online account from the game's Settings at any time.
        </p>
      </div>

      <h2>Who this is from</h2>
      <p>
        {site.name} is made by {site.person}, an independent developer publishing as
        {site.studio}. "We" and "us" on this page mean {site.person}. This policy
        covers the {site.name} app for iPhone and Android, the game server it talks to,
        and the website at playhatchfire.com.
      </p>

      <h2>Playing offline</h2>
      <p>
        Your progress is saved in a file on your device. Played offline, it isn't sent
        to us or to anyone else. Because it's part of the app's data, it may be included
        in your own device backups if you've turned those on. Deleting the app deletes
        the save.
      </p>

      <h2>Playing online</h2>
      <p>
        Cloud saves, Waymeet (the town you share with other players), friends, player
        cards, raids and world events need our game server. They're optional: the game
        asks before you use them, and plays on without them.
      </p>

      <h3>Your account</h3>
      <p>
        The first time you go online, the game makes an account on our server, known by
        a random ID the app creates. It isn't your phone's hardware ID. If you sign in
        with Apple or Google, that account is linked so your guild can follow you to
        another phone. Apple or Google tells our server an ID for your account with
        them, and may pass on the name or email address you chose to share. We use these
        only to sign you in.
      </p>

      <h3>What the server keeps</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Your guild's save</strong>, so it can be restored on another phone, and
          a few earlier copies (up to ten, about an hour apart) in case a save goes wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your player card</strong>: your leader's and guild's names, their look,
          crest and banner, your dragon, your hall's level, the counts you've earned
          and the two things you pin. Other players can see it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your hall</strong>, without anything private in it, so your friends can
          visit it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Friends</strong>: your friends, requests, and anyone you've blocked.
          Friends see when you last played.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Online play</strong>: what you've earned from world events and work
          parties until the game collects it, and the moves a raid or a sparring bout
          needs while it's on.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your birth year and that you accepted the chat rules</strong>, if you
          give them to type in Waymeet's chat. The year can't be changed once given.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Moderation</strong>: reports, a moderator's actions (a name reset, a
          muted chat, a suspension) and the reason for them.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Chat</h3>
      <p>
        Waymeet's chat is passed between the players in the same copy of the town and
        run through a word filter. It isn't stored with your account. The server holds
        the last lines said in each copy of the town for a short while, so that if
        someone reports a player, the lines around it can be kept with the report for a
        moderator to read. Lines you send are shown to other players with your leader's
        name.
      </p>

      <h3>Connections</h3>
      <p>
        Like any online service, the server processes your IP address and your app's
        version to connect you and keep the service secure. Our server is reached
        through Cloudflare, which handles that traffic on our behalf. See
        <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/privacypolicy/" rel="noopener">Cloudflare's privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h3>How long it's kept</h3>
      <p>
        Your account and what it holds are kept while the account exists. When you
        delete it, your save, card, hall, friends and sign-in links go with it, and an
        Apple sign-in is cancelled too. Reports and moderators' notes about an account
        may be kept a while longer, so a moderator can still act on them. Copies in
        our server's backups are replaced as new backups are made.
      </p>

      <h2>What the game doesn't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>No advertising and no advertising identifiers.</li>
        <li>No analytics, tracking or crash-reporting software of our own.</li>
        <li>No access to your contacts, photos, location, camera or microphone.</li>
        <li>No selling or renting of anything about you.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Notifications</h2>
      <p>
        If you allow them, the game reminds you when a party is home, a cart has
        arrived and so on. These are scheduled on your device by the game itself. No
        server is involved, and you can turn each kind off in the game's Settings or
        all of them in your phone's Settings.
      </p>

      <h2>The purchase</h2>
      <p>
        The game has one optional purchase, which opens the full game. Apple (on iPhone)
        or Google (on Android) handles the payment. We never see your name, card or
        billing details. The game is only told whether your Apple or Google account owns
        the purchase, and it keeps that answer in your save. See
        <a href="https://www.apple.com/legal/privacy/" rel="noopener">Apple's privacy policy</a>
        and
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Google's privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Test versions</h2>
      <p>
        If you test the game through Apple's TestFlight or Google Play's testing tracks,
        Apple or Google passes on what you choose to send us: written feedback,
        screenshots and crash reports, along with basic details such as your device
        model and system version. If you were invited by email, we have the address the
        invitation went to. We use these only to fix the game and to reach you about
        testing, and we delete tester details when testing ends or when you ask.
      </p>

      <h2>What the stores show us</h2>
      <p>
        Apple and Google give every developer sales and download totals, and usage and
        crash figures from people who have agreed to share them with developers. These
        are grouped together and don't identify you.
      </p>

      <h2>This website</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Hosting.</strong> The site is served by Cloudflare. Like any web host,
          Cloudflare processes your IP address and browser details to deliver pages and
          to keep the site secure.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Newsletter.</strong> If you sign up, your email address is stored by
          our email provider, Kit. We use it only to send news about {site.name}. Every
          email has an unsubscribe link. The sign-up form is loaded from Kit's servers,
          so Kit receives the usual details of that request. See
          <a href="https://kit.com/privacy" rel="noopener">Kit's privacy policy</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No ads or trackers.</strong> We don't run advertising, and we don't set
          cookies to follow you between sites.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Links.</strong> Discord, X, Reddit, the wiki's host and the app stores
          are run by other companies and follow their own policies.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Sharing</h2>
      <p>
        We don't sell personal information and we don't share it for advertising. The
        only companies involved are the ones named above (Apple, Google, Cloudflare and
        Kit), each for the job described. Other players see only what's described above
        as shown to them: your card, your chat lines, and, for friends, your hall and
        when you last played.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>
        {site.name} isn't directed at children under 13. In Waymeet's chat, anyone who
        says they're under 13, or doesn't give a birth year, can only send and see the
        game's own set phrases, never typed messages. If you believe a child has given
        us personal information, tell us and we'll delete it.
      </p>

      <h2>Your choices</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Play offline: nothing is sent to us.</li>
        <li>Turn cloud saves off in the game; your player card is taken down with them.</li>
        <li>
          Delete your online account in the game's Settings. This removes it from our
          server; the guild on your phone stays.
        </li>
        <li>To erase your game data on your phone, delete the app.</li>
        <li>To leave the newsletter, use the link in any email.</li>
        <li>
          To see, correct or delete anything we hold about you, ask us. Depending on
          where you live, the law may give you further rights over your personal
          information, and we'll honour them.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If the game or the site starts handling information differently, this page will
        change first and the date at the top will be updated.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>Questions about privacy: reach us at <Contact />.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
